##
    When something goes wrong.

    An exception is for the case a function cannot answer: not "the answer is nothing", which
    is an optional, but "there is no answer and I cannot go on". Telling those two apart is
    most of using exceptions well, and the last section here is about exactly that.

    Everything else is small. A program declares an exception by extending one, throws it,
    and catches it by naming it or any of its ancestors.
##

# ---- Declaring some ------------------------------------------------------------------

##
    Extending Exception is all it takes. The constructor passes a message up with base(...),
    which is what Message() gives back later — Exception declares nothing else a program can
    see, so the message is the one thing every exception carries.
##
model KitchenProblem extends Exception
    public function KitchenProblem(string message)
        base(message);
    end function
end model

##
    Exceptions form a hierarchy like any other model, and it is worth one: a caller who cares
    which thing went wrong names the exact one, and a caller who only cares that something did
    names the parent. Neither has to know about the other.
##
model OutOfIngredient extends KitchenProblem
    public string Ingredient;

    public function OutOfIngredient(string ingredient)
        base("no " + ingredient + " left");
        this.Ingredient = ingredient;
    end function
end model

model OvenBroken extends KitchenProblem
    public function OvenBroken()
        base("the oven will not light");
    end function
end model

global model Program

    function Main()
        Program.ThrowingAndCatching();
        Program.CatchingByAncestor();
        Program.WhichClauseWins();
        Program.FinallyRunsEitherWay();
        Program.PassingItOn();
        Program.TheOnesTheLanguageRaises();
        Program.WhenNotToThrow();
    end function

    # ---- The shape of it ----------------------------------------------------------------

    function Bake(string ingredient)
        if ingredient == "flour"
            throw new OutOfIngredient("flour");
        end if

        Console.WriteLine("  baked with {{ingredient}}");
    end function

    function ThrowingAndCatching()
        Console.WriteLine("== throwing and catching ==");

        try
            Program.Bake("sugar");
            Program.Bake("flour");
            Console.WriteLine("  never reached");
        catch OutOfIngredient problem
            Console.WriteLine("  {{problem.Message()}}");

            # A declared exception may carry whatever it likes besides the message.
            Console.WriteLine("  the missing one was {{problem.Ingredient}}");
        end try
    end function

    # ---- Catching a parent --------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        A catch takes the type it names and anything descending from it, so one clause handles
        a whole family. 'catch Exception' takes every exception there is, declared or not.
    ##
    function CatchingByAncestor()
        Console.WriteLine("== catching a parent ==");

        try
            throw new OvenBroken();
        catch KitchenProblem problem
            Console.WriteLine("  something in the kitchen: {{problem.Message()}}");
        end try

        try
            throw new OutOfIngredient("eggs");
        catch Exception problem
            Console.WriteLine("  something, anything: {{problem.Message()}}");
        end try
    end function

    # ---- Several clauses ------------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        The first clause that matches wins, so the particular ones go above the general ones.
        Written the other way round, a 'catch Exception' at the top would take everything and
        nothing below it could ever run.
    ##
    function WhichClauseWins()
        Console.WriteLine("== which clause wins ==");

        Program.Attempt(new OutOfIngredient("butter"));
        Program.Attempt(new OvenBroken());
        Program.Attempt(new Exception("something else entirely"));
    end function

    function Attempt(Exception what)
        try
            throw what;
        catch OutOfIngredient problem
            Console.WriteLine("  shopping: {{problem.Ingredient}}");
        catch KitchenProblem problem
            Console.WriteLine("  kitchen:  {{problem.Message()}}");
        catch Exception problem
            Console.WriteLine("  other:    {{problem.Message()}}");
        end try
    end function

    # ---- finally ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        Runs whichever way the block was left: off the end, through a catch, or on the way out
        of a throw nothing here caught. It is where the things that must happen anyway go.
    ##
    function FinallyRunsEitherWay()
        Console.WriteLine("== finally ==");

        try
            Console.WriteLine("  nothing wrong");
        finally
            Console.WriteLine("  and the kitchen is tidied");
        end try

        try
            throw new OvenBroken();
        catch KitchenProblem problem
            Console.WriteLine("  caught it");
        finally
            Console.WriteLine("  and the kitchen is tidied again");
        end try
    end function

    # ---- Handing it further up --------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        A catch that cannot really deal with the problem can say so by throwing again. There is
        no bare 'throw': the one that was caught is a value with a name, so it is thrown the way
        anything is thrown.

        Catching something only to hide it is the mistake this exists to avoid.
    ##
    function PassingItOn()
        Console.WriteLine("== handing it on ==");

        try
            Program.NoteAndRethrow();
        catch KitchenProblem problem
            Console.WriteLine("  the caller dealt with it: {{problem.Message()}}");
        end try
    end function

    function NoteAndRethrow()
        try
            throw new OutOfIngredient("salt");
        catch OutOfIngredient problem
            Console.WriteLine("  noted, but this is not mine to fix");
            throw problem;
        end try
    end function

    # ---- The ones the language raises ---------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        A program does not throw all of these itself; the language raises them when something
        goes wrong that it could not see while compiling. Each is caught the same way.

        A function never declares what it may throw, and nothing forces a caller to handle it.
        That is deliberate: the alternative teaches people to write a catch that swallows.
    ##
    function TheOnesTheLanguageRaises()
        Console.WriteLine("== the ones the language raises ==");

        integer[] three = {1, 2, 3};
        integer zero = 0;
        integer? nothing;

        integer count = 5;

        Program.Show(function() let a = 1 / zero; end function);
        Program.Show(function() let b = three[7]; end function);
        Program.Show(function() let c = nothing.Value(); end function);
        Program.Show(function() let d = 2147483647 * 2147483647 * 4; end function);
        Program.Show(function() let e = count.Format("Q9"); end function);
    end function

    ##
        Runs something and says which exception came out, if any.

        The parameter is written 'delegate()' rather than 'Function'. Function is the root
        every function type descends from — it says a value is a function without saying what
        shape, and a shape is exactly what calling one needs.
    ##
    function Show(delegate() attempt)
        try
            attempt();
            Console.WriteLine("  nothing went wrong");
        catch DivideByZeroException problem
            Console.WriteLine("  divided by zero");
        catch IndexOutOfRangeException problem
            Console.WriteLine("  reached past the end");
        catch EmptyOptionalException problem
            Console.WriteLine("  read an empty optional");
        catch OverflowException problem
            Console.WriteLine("  too big to hold");
        catch FormatException problem
            Console.WriteLine("  a pattern that means nothing");
        end try
    end function

    # ---- When not to throw ------------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        The question worth asking every time: is this an answer, or the absence of one?

        Looking for something and not finding it is an answer, and an optional says it. A
        caller then has to deal with the empty case before it can read the value, which is the
        checking a catch would have made optional.

        Throwing is for the case where there is no answer to give and going on would be worse
        than stopping.
    ##
    function WhenNotToThrow()
        Console.WriteLine("== an answer, or the absence of one ==");

        string[] shelf = {"flour", "sugar"};

        # Not finding it is ordinary, so this yields nothing rather than throwing.
        Console.WriteLine("  looking for sugar: {{Program.Find(shelf, "sugar").Or("not there")}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  looking for salt:  {{Program.Find(shelf, "salt").Or("not there")}}");

        # Being asked for a negative amount is not an answer at all.
        try
            Program.Weigh(-1);
        catch ArgumentException problem
            Console.WriteLine("  {{problem.Message()}}");
        end try
    end function

    string? function Find(string[] shelf, string what)
        for each item in shelf
            if item == what
                yield item;
            end if
        end for

        string? missing;
        yield missing;
    end function

    function Weigh(integer grams)
        if grams < 0
            throw new ArgumentException("cannot weigh " + grams + " grams");
        end if

        Console.WriteLine("  weighed {{grams}}g");
    end function

end model
